                                                                                "summary":  "Brown profiles Ron Baron\u0027s remarkable 40-year track record, during which Baron Capital grew from $10 million to roughly $70 billion in AUM by holding concentrated long-term positions in transformative companies. Key wins include Tesla (entered at a split-adjusted ~$14/share in 2014, now a $5B position) and SpaceX (now a $25B stake). Baron\u0027s current prediction is that SpaceX could reach a $20 trillion valuation, making it the world\u0027s largest company. Brown frames the lesson as the power of conviction-based, decade-long holdingÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Âignoring mainstream skepticism in favor of rigorous business analysis.",
                                                                                "take":  "The Baron profile is instructive but carries a survivorship bias caveat Brown doesn\u0027t emphasize enough: for every manager who held Tesla through 10 drawdowns of 30%+, dozens of others held Theranos or WeWork through similar volatility with a different ending. The genuine takeaway is not hold forever but hold when the underlying business continues to compoundÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚ÂBaron\u0027s edge was not stubbornness, it was ongoing fundamental re-conviction at each inflection. That distinction matters enormously for investors who might apply this lesson to the wrong name.",
